The values are always reduced modulo `100000`, so each residue can be treated as a graph node. From a state `value`, one move goes to `(value * factor) % 100000` for every factor in `arr`.

A normal BFS gives the fewest moves because every multiplication has the same cost. The distance array also prevents the same residue from being added again.

```cpp
class Solution{
public:
    int minimumMultiplications(vector<int> &arr,
                               int start, int end) {
        const int MOD = 100000;
        vector<int> distance(MOD, -1);
        queue<int> q;

        distance[start] = 0;
        q.push(start);

        while (!q.empty()) {
            int value = q.front();
            q.pop();

            if (value == end) {
                return distance[value];
            }

            for (int factor : arr) {
                int nextValue = (int)((long long)value * factor % MOD);

                if (distance[nextValue] == -1) {
                    distance[nextValue] = distance[value] + 1;
                    q.push(nextValue);
                }
            }
        }

        return -1;
    }
};
```

For example, with `arr = [2, 3]`, `start = 1`, and `end = 9`, BFS can use `1 -> 3 -> 9`, so the answer is `2`.

Time complexity: `O(100000 * arr.length)` in the worst case.
Space complexity: `O(100000)`.

This version searches from both sides. Forward moves are easy to generate. For the reverse search, it finds every value `x` that can produce `target` through `x * factor % 100000`.

For a factor `a`, let `g = gcd(a, 100000)`. A reverse value exists only when `target` is divisible by `g`. The reduced modular equation is solved with a modular inverse, and all possible residue solutions are checked.

```java
class Solution {
    private static final int MOD = 100000;

    public int minimumMultiplications(int[] arr, int start, int end) {
        if (start == end) {
            return 0;
        }

        int[] fromStart = new int[MOD];
        int[] fromEnd = new int[MOD];
        java.util.Arrays.fill(fromStart, -1);
        java.util.Arrays.fill(fromEnd, -1);

        java.util.ArrayList<Integer> left = new java.util.ArrayList<>();
        java.util.ArrayList<Integer> right = new java.util.ArrayList<>();
        left.add(start);
        right.add(end);
        fromStart[start] = 0;
        fromEnd[end] = 0;

        while (!left.isEmpty() && !right.isEmpty()) {
            if (left.size() <= right.size()) {
                java.util.ArrayList<Integer> next = new java.util.ArrayList<>();

                for (int value : left) {
                    for (int factor : arr) {
                        int nextValue = (int)((long) value * factor % MOD);

                        if (fromStart[nextValue] == -1) {
                            fromStart[nextValue] = fromStart[value] + 1;
                            if (fromEnd[nextValue] != -1) {
                                return fromStart[nextValue] + fromEnd[nextValue];
                            }
                            next.add(nextValue);
                        }
                    }
                }
                left = next;
            } else {
                java.util.ArrayList<Integer> next = new java.util.ArrayList<>();

                for (int target : right) {
                    for (int factor : arr) {
                        int a = factor % MOD;
                        int g = gcd(a, MOD);

                        if (target % g != 0) {
                            continue;
                        }

                        if (g == MOD) {
                            if (target != 0) {
                                continue;
                            }
                            for (int previous = 0; previous < MOD; previous++) {
                                if (fromEnd[previous] == -1) {
                                    fromEnd[previous] = fromEnd[target] + 1;
                                    if (fromStart[previous] != -1) {
                                        return fromEnd[previous] + fromStart[previous];
                                    }
                                    next.add(previous);
                                }
                            }
                            continue;
                        }

                        int reducedMod = MOD / g;
                        int reducedA = a / g;
                        int reducedTarget = target / g;
                        int inverse = modInverse(reducedA, reducedMod);
                        int first = (int)((long) reducedTarget * inverse % reducedMod);

                        for (int k = 0; k < g; k++) {
                            int previous = first + k * reducedMod;

                            if (fromEnd[previous] == -1) {
                                fromEnd[previous] = fromEnd[target] + 1;
                                if (fromStart[previous] != -1) {
                                    return fromEnd[previous] + fromStart[previous];
                                }
                                next.add(previous);
                            }
                        }
                    }
                }
                right = next;
            }
        }

        return -1;
    }

    private int gcd(int a, int b) {
        while (b != 0) {
            int temp = a % b;
            a = b;
            b = temp;
        }
        return a;
    }

    private int modInverse(int value, int mod) {
        long oldR = value;
        long r = mod;
        long oldS = 1;
        long s = 0;

        while (r != 0) {
            long quotient = oldR / r;

            long tempR = oldR - quotient * r;
            oldR = r;
            r = tempR;

            long tempS = oldS - quotient * s;
            oldS = s;
            s = tempS;
        }

        return (int)((oldS % mod + mod) % mod);
    }
}
```

The special factor `0` is handled separately. It can only produce target `0`, and every residue is a possible previous state in that case. If the two searches never meet, the target cannot be reached.

Time complexity depends on the number of reverse candidates generated. In the worst case it is `O(100000 * arr.length)`.
Space complexity: `O(100000)`.

This keeps the BFS distance inside the queue as `[value, moves]`. It is a little more direct than keeping a separate distance array, and the `Uint8Array` is enough for visited flags because every state only needs an unseen or seen value.

```javascript
class Solution {
    minimumMultiplications(arr, start, end) {
        const MOD = 100000;

        if (start === end) {
            return 0;
        }

        const factors = [...new Set(arr)];
        const visited = new Uint8Array(MOD);
        const queue = [[start, 0]];
        let front = 0;
        visited[start] = 1;

        while (front < queue.length) {
            const [value, moves] = queue[front++];

            for (const factor of factors) {
                const nextValue = (value * factor) % MOD;

                if (nextValue === end) {
                    return moves + 1;
                }

                if (visited[nextValue] === 0) {
                    visited[nextValue] = 1;
                    queue.push([nextValue, moves + 1]);
                }
            }
        }

        return -1;
    }
}
```

There are only `100000` possible states because of the modulo operation. A value such as `100000` becomes `0` after multiplication and reduction, so the fixed state range also handles that case correctly.

Time complexity: `O(100000 * U)`, where `U` is the number of distinct values in `arr`.
Space complexity: `O(100000 + U)`.

All edges have the same cost, so BFS is usually the simpler choice. This implementation uses Dijkstra's algorithm anyway. It still works because the queue always removes the state with the smallest number of multiplications seen so far.

The distance array is needed because a residue can be reached in different ways. When a shorter distance is found, the state is inserted again with its improved priority.

```csharp
class Solution {
    public int minimumMultiplications(int[] arr, int start, int end) {
        const int MOD = 100000;

        if (start == end) {
            return 0;
        }

        int[] distance = new int[MOD];
        Array.Fill(distance, int.MaxValue);
        distance[start] = 0;

        var queue = new PriorityQueue<int, int>();
        queue.Enqueue(start, 0);

        while (queue.Count > 0) {
            int value = queue.Dequeue();
            int currentDistance = distance[value];

            if (value == end) {
                return currentDistance;
            }

            foreach (int factor in arr) {
                int nextValue = (int)((long)value * factor % MOD);
                int nextDistance = currentDistance + 1;

                if (nextDistance < distance[nextValue]) {
                    distance[nextValue] = nextDistance;
                    queue.Enqueue(nextValue, nextDistance);
                }
            }
        }

        return -1;
    }
}
```

If `arr` contains `0`, the product correctly moves to state `0`. If no sequence reaches `end`, the priority queue becomes empty and the method returns `-1`.

Time complexity: `O(100000 * arr.Length * log(100000))` in the worst case.
Space complexity: `O(100000)`.

