class Solution {
   public:
    int minimumMultiplications(vector<int> &arr, int start, int end) {
        int MOD=1e5;
        if (start == end) return 0;
        vector<int> dist(100000, -1);
        int n=arr.size();
        queue<int>q;
        q.push(start);
        dist[start] = 0;
        while (!q.empty()) {
            int node = q.front();
            q.pop();
            for (int i=0;i<n;i++) {
                int elem =(node*arr[i])%MOD;
                if(elem==end) return dist[node]+1;
                if(dist[elem]==-1){
                    dist[elem]=dist[node]+1;
                    q.push(elem);
                }

            }
        }
        return -1;
    }
};

```

## Intuition
If start = end, no steps needed so return 0. Else, start with a steps array of length 10^5 and initialize all the cells as Integer.MAX_VALUE (i.e. unreachable at the beginning). Push the start value in a queue and set steps[start] to 0. Thereafter perform BFS on the nodes pushed in the queue whenever a smaller number of steps makes them reachable by multiplying the current node value with one of the array elements.

## Approach
1. BASE CASE : If start = end, return 0.
2. Create a steps array of length 10^5 (so that it can accommodate 0 - 99999 value cells) to store minimum steps to reach a node initialized to infinity for all possible values. Set the distance of the starting value (start) to 0. Use a queue to perform BFS.
3. Perform BFS traversal starting from the source node (start value).
For each node(value), update the distance of its adjacent nodes(values) if a shorter path is found and push the node in the queue. Return the steps taken if end value is reached.
4. If reaching end value is not possible, return -1.

## Solution
```java[]
class Solution {

    private final int RANGE = 100000;

    public int minimumMultiplications(int[] arr, int start, int end) {
        if(start == end) return 0;

        int[] steps = new int[RANGE];
        Arrays.fill(steps, Integer.MAX_VALUE);

        Queue<Integer> q = new LinkedList<>();
        q.offer(start);   
        steps[start] = 0;

        while(!q.isEmpty()) {
            int curr = q.poll();
            int step = steps[curr];

            for(int i=0; i<arr.length; i++) {
                int prod = (curr * arr[i]) % RANGE;

                if(steps[prod] > step + 1) {
                    steps[prod] = step + 1;
                    q.offer(prod);
                }

                if(prod == end) return steps[prod];
            }
        }

        return steps[end] == Integer.MAX_VALUE ? -1 : steps[end];
    }
}


```

## Complexity
**Time Complexity:** 
O(M * N), where M = 100000 is the constant MOD and N is the number of elements in arr. Each of the M states is added to the queue at most once, and for each state, we iterate through N multipliers.

**Space Complexity:**
O(M), where M = 100000. The steps array takes O(M) space, and the queue can hold up to M unique states.
